Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"Aggregating many imperfect judgments can outperform the judgment of experts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If each member of a group is more likely than not to judge correctly and judges independently, the probability that the majority is right approaches certainty as the group grows, which is the formal core of the case for democratic over expert decision.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the strongest epistemic argument against rule by the competent, since it grants the premise that competence matters and still favours the crowd.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Three replies are available to the defender of expert rule. One may deny that the model applies, since real electorates are neither independent nor reliably better than chance. One may concede the theorem and argue that a competence-weighted electorate captures the same aggregation benefit without the noise. Or one may argue that weighting the more informed makes the aggregate converge faster, so the theorem supports rule by the competent rather than undermining it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism\">A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>A different result from the same tradition, concerning which outcome is selected rather than how reliable it is.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people\">Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people</a><ul><li>The claim that this argument is meant to answer.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Plural voting admits no unique assignment of votes\">Plural voting admits no unique assignment of votes</a><ul><li>A defect in the competence-weighting reply.</li></ul></li></ul>","Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it is commonly claimed that low-income individuals are more likely to suffer from subjective well-being and mental health deficiencies, a recent meta-study has shown that this is not the case. There is no correlation between inequality and mental health being undermined.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the common view that inequality contributes to greater mental health problems and lower subjective well-being.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>This does not mean that low-income individuals do not always experience greater mental health problems and lower subjective well-being. It is simply stating that the link between these measures are not as clear and robust as one might initially think.</p><h4>References</h4>","Endurance is a crucial component of courage":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Endurance is the ability to withstand pain (whereas continence is the ability to resist the temptation of pleasure). It is closely related to courage, since to the courage, one must first be enduring.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains what endurance is, and what it is important for.</p><h4>Softness &amp; Insensitivity</h4><p>The deficient state of endurance is softness, or an oversensitivity to pain. Such a person flees all hardship, and does not stand their ground. The excessive state of endurance is insensitivity. Such a person experiences pain for no reason.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be courageous is not to be afraid of death\">To be courageous is not to be afraid of death</a><ul><li>To not be afraid of death means not being afraid of pain.</li></ul></li></ul>","Humanity is insignificant":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that humanity is insignificant in the vastness of existence. This is a core belief in philosophical schools such as the existentialists.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea in many existentialist schools of philosophy and works of visual art and literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In the plays of Sophocles, such as Oedipus the King, Oedipus at Colonus, or Antigone, humanity is always portrayed as entirely subservient to the gods, as they are essentially bent to the will of the gods in their every action. <ul><li>In fact, the entire plot of Oedipus, first of him as king, and later, when he was at Colonus, was essentially dictated by the gods.<ul><li>If Oedipus does not find out about the truth of his family, the city will continue to suffer from the plague. He is forced, then, to find out the truth.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus at Colonus\">Oedipus at Colonus</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The world is unfair and amoral\">The world is unfair and amoral</a><ul><li>This is a common theme or deduction based on this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Hungary's economy is suffering from a lack of demand, deficit spending, and corruption":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Hungary is suffering an economic downturn due to a lack of demand for their products, their excessive deficit spending, and the corruption in Orban’s government.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This economic downturn could potentially end Orban’s regime in Hungary.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Polish economic miracle is largely a result of EU benefits and a diversified economy\">The Polish economic miracle is largely a result of EU benefits and a diversified economy</a></li></ul>","It is not good for man to be alone":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Given my God to Adam in the Garden of Eden, God says how people ought to live in a community, and not just isolate themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a commandment that God gives.</p><h4>Bible</h4><ul><li>This is a key insight into what it means to be human, since it is NOT GOOD that man is alone. <ul><li>This is a contrast with the “and it was good” form the previous verses.</li></ul></li><li>Man is set up as a mediator between the earthly and the heavenly, answerable to both God and other humans.</li><li>A person’s identity is defined in terms of his relationship with others. <ul><li>Adam is only able to name himself “ish” after he names Even “isha”</li></ul></li></ul><h4><em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></h4><p>This commandment relates to the idea that Dostoevsky explicates in <em>TBK</em>, where he essentially advocates for Christian Socialism and existentializes the process of confession into something earthly, specifically, public/group confession. He also states explicitly that isolation from others (especially as a result of sin/guilt) is the existentialized version of hell.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Brothers Karamazov\">The Brothers Karamazov</a></li></ul>","Magnanimity is the 'crown' of the moral virtues":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To be magnanimous means to have every other virtue. Additionally, it means knowing that you are virtuous in every way, and acting in an apt manner. The magnanimous person cares (in the lightest sense of the word) only for honor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the most interesting of Aristotle’s virtues, since it is the encapsulation of all other virtues in one.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>The deficient vice of magnanimity is being pusillanimous, and the excessive vice is being conceitful. The magnanimous man does not particularly care about honor, though he accepts honors from good people (since it is what he ought to receive), but shuns honors from lower people (since they are irrelevant to him). He views external events as beneath his dignity, and thus does not react irrationality when circumstances outside his control falter. Additionally, to achieve this virtue means having access to power, good birth, and wealth. However, power and wealth only matter to him because of the honor that are associated with having them.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth\">Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth</a><ul><li>Like magnificence, magnanimity is a virtue only achievable for some.</li></ul></li></ul>","Moral principles originate from rational choice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the basis for all contractarian theories, whereby what is moral is decided through collective bargaining.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the underpinning of contractarian moral through.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This essentially reduces morality to rational decision making, which is an idea that some find appealing, while others reject. Theories that are in this vein include Rawls’ Contractualism, Contractarianism, and it is even related to Kant’s theories, since Kant argues that morality is something that people <em>rationally</em> agree upon.</p><h4>References</h4>","NAFTA increased mortality and lower living standards in certain areas of the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although aggregate statistics calculated that there was a +0.11% real wage gain as a result of NAFTA trade, this overlooks the localized negative consequences.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the importance of looking at issues that are localized and making appropriate judgements with such information.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There was a sharp increase in mortality when trade barriers are lifted for manufacturing sectors especially.</p><ul><li>Following 1994, there was a 0.68% increase in annual mortality for areas that were exposed to NAFTA.</li><li>This burden fell most heavily on working age men (8.9% increased in mortality for them).<ul><li>Ripple effect for widows.</li></ul></li><li>There are higher levels of reported “fair/poor health,” higher levels of smoking, and a surge in Medicare coverage among the affected demographic.</li><li>Accounting for all of this, the net welfare is actually a loss (-0.45% of annual consumption for a 45-year-old male).</li></ul><p>Trade shocks specifically effect people in manufacturing sectors, and cause adverse effects. There are studies that show how recessions actually make people healthier, but recessions (such as the one in 2008), typically largely affect service and non-manufacturing roles.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved\">Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved</a><ul><li>This note would contradict this linked note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Pure time preference is irrational":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of pure time preference, or that we value something less merely because it is in the future (not taking account things such as the increased risk of the future or other factors like that) is irrational.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for specific argument in population ethics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that we could value all period of our life the same. There is no reason to do otherwise. This also implies that we should not discount future civilizations, and thus we should not over consume now and doom future generations.</p><ul><li>But it seems that many of the disregarded factors do play a role here. For instance, it seems likely that the longer in the future, the likelier that humanity will go extinct. Therefore, it seems makes sense to discount.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rule of law is better than rule by decree since laws are cold and objective, meaning that it is incorruptible, unlike men’s souls.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s argument for the rule of law.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>This seems to be the beginning of the traditional of constitutional liberalism in Western government.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a><ul><li>The rule of law can exist independently from democracy, and this was already identified even in Aristotle’s works.</li></ul></li></ul>","Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Someone who is temperate is someone who actively seeks pleasure, but they do not pain when there is no pleasure. They take pleasure in the right things (they desire the right things), in the right amount, at the right time, and in the right way.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a part of Aristotle’s analysis of what is means to be various virtues.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>While the temperate man desires the right things, the continent man desires the wrong things (though he is able to overcome such a incorrect desire with rationality).</li></ul></li></ul>","The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that one’s suffering is brought upon ultimately by oneself. A major theme in works such as Hamlet, and the Greek Tragedies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has been a common theme for many works of philosophy and literature throughout the ages and is a crucial aspect of the human condition as explored through works of literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>This is a prominent theme in <em>Hamlet</em>, as Hamlet is forced to suffer through the murder of his father, and his own indecision, being unable to take revenge.<ul><li>This indecision is likely due to several factors, including his unsureness about the nature of the ghost that informs him of the murder of his father, his intellectualism (he was a scholar at the University of Wittenburg) leading to over-analyzation and paralysis of action, and his psychological complexity, related to his potential Oedipus complex.</li></ul></li><li>This is likewise a prominent theme in the Sophoclean tragedies, for example, <em>Oedipus the King</em>, where Oedipus is forced advance his own down fall unknowingly, suffering all the way along.</li><li>This is also the case with both Victor and the creature throughout <em>Frankenstein</em>. <ul><li>Victor is forced to constantly suffer the result of his own creation, his creature, as the creature terrorizes him and murders the ones that he loves. </li><li>At the same time, the creature feels unloved due to his appearance, turning to violence as a means of attempting to achieve catharsis with his inner torment. Yet, this violence does not serve to mitigate his suffering; rather, it worsens his suffering tormenting him with remorse.<ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Blasted as thou wert, my agony was still superior to thine; for the bitter sting of remorse will not cease to rankle in my wounds until death shall close them for ever.</span></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Savings and Loan Crisis was caused by interest rate shocks, risky investments, and regulatory failure":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Savings and Loans Crisis was caused by interest shocks, risky investments by the thrifts, and the slowness to act of regulatory institutions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The failure of the Savings and Loan Crisis can teach us something about how to ensure that a market does not fail do to external pressure, and how to regulate markets well.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The surge in inflation during the late 1970s led the Fed to raise rates significantly, which meant that thrift had to pay much higher rates, driving their profit margins into the ground. Deregulation during the period also led to the thrifts making much more risky and speculative investments, which meant that they were at a higher chance of failure when their investments failed. The regulatory institutions also failed in their attempt to ensure the stability of the market, as they were underfunded and bureaucratic, slow to update regulation to adapt to changing circumstances.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li></ul>","The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Weber makes the claims that the entire history of monasticism, that is, the ascetic practice, can be frame as strings of attempts to secularize the influence of wealth and detach it from religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Weber uses this to contrast the Protestant attitude: Protestants essentially began viewing wealth through the lens of religion, in a sense incorporating it into their belief system. This can be seen through their belief that making money is a sign of God’s blessing (when the money is not wasted).</p><h4>Quote</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">John Wesley</p><p>I fear, wherever riches have increased, the essence of religion has decreased in the same proportion… For religion must necessarily produce both industry and frugality… But as riches increase, so will pride, anger, and love of the world…</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","To be just, one must have a disposition towards just action and be able to make deliberate choices":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In order for one to be just, one has to continually practice adopting a just disposition, and one must be in control of their actions. The opposite is also true for injustice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle explaining the conditions that must be met in order for someone to be considered just.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It is important to note that just doing an unjust thing once does not make someone unjust. The unjust actions have to be both sustained and deliberate by the person in order for the person to be unjust, and vice versa.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>This is related to the idea that virtue must be repeatedly practiced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>The agent not only needs to be able to perform just actions, they must be able in a just disposition in order for them to be considered just.</li></ul></li></ul>","Utilitarianism (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A defense of the moral theory that actions are right in proportion as they promote happiness and wrong as they produce the reverse of happiness. In the book, Mill explains that happiness means pleasure and the absence of pain, but he also argues that pleasures differ in quality, not just quantity, so intellectual and moral pleasures are superior to purely bodily ones. He responds to common objections by showing that utilitarianism is not a crude doctrine of selfish indulgence, and he defends it as a foundation for morality, justice, and social improvement, emphasizing that the best moral rules are those that tend to maximize overall human well-being.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the foundational works which explicates the theory of utilitarianism.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Issues involving justice involve high levels of utility\">Issues involving justice involve high levels of utility</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological\">All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good does not need to be ‘proved’\">What is good does not need to be ‘proved’</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life\">Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some pleasures are greater than others\">Some pleasures are greater than others</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While its easier for less advanced animals to be satisfied, the more advanced can make the imperfection bearable\">While its easier for less advanced animals to be satisfied, the more advanced can make the imperfection bearable</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We shouldn’t calculate utility before each action, but rather construct rules for generally beneficial actions\">We shouldn’t calculate utility before each action, but rather construct rules for generally beneficial actions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Law is not constitutive of justice\">Law is not constitutive of justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is an individual’s moral right\">Justice is an individual’s moral right</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having\">A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>This book attacks utilitarianism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>Presents an alternative theory to utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
